to the Probity Trust in lower Manhattan, dining with fellow clerks in crowded restaurants, and studying investments in the quiet Yale Club library, often interrupted by rowdy patrons who never entered the reading room. After work, he walks past the old Murray Hill Hotel and along Madison Avenue to Pennsylvania Station, finding both exhilaration and loneliness in the city’s nocturnal energy—imagining romantic connections with strangers on Fifth Avenue and feeling a shared melancholy among young clerks lingering in twilight. He reconnects with Jordan Baker in midsummer, initially drawn by her fame as a golf champion, but later recognizing her dishonesty after learning she cheated in a tournament and lied about damaging a car, yet he remains emotionally detached, acknowledging his own moral stance as one of the few honest people he knows.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Nick (narrator)</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Jordan Baker</span>\n                    \n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            \n\n            <h3 style=\"margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--accent-soft);\">Chapters</h3>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 1</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">14,030 words &middot; 1h 33m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with Nick Carraway arriving by car in West Egg, feeling lonely until a stranger’s request for directions gives him purpose; he reflects on his ambitions for self-reinvention and notes the geography of West Egg and East Egg, separated by a bay, with his modest house between two grand mansions, one belonging to the enigmatic Gatsby. He visits his cousin Daisy and husband Tom Buchanan in their opulent East Egg Georgian Colonial, where he meets Jordan Baker and witnesses Tom’s violent outburst—Daisy reveals a bruised knuckle, accusing Tom of harming her. Later, on a rosy porch at dusk, Daisy and Tom engage in tense banter, Tom cites racist ideology from The Rise of the Coloured Empires, and Jordan reveals Tom’s mistress in New York; Daisy’s hollow confession that she hopes her daughter will be a 'beautiful little fool' underscores her disillusionment. Nick accompanies Tom by train to the valley of ashes, where they meet Myrtle Wilson at her husband George’s garage; Myrtle, vibrant yet trapped, travels to New York with Tom in separate cars, impulsively buying a dog, magazines, and perfume. In Myrtle’s cramped Manhattan apartment, a drunken party erupts with guests including her sister Catherine and the McKees; Catherine claims Daisy is preventing Tom’s divorce because she is Catholic—a lie—and Myrtle recounts a romanticized train meeting with Tom. The party descends into chaos when Tom violently breaks Myrtle’s nose after she shouts Daisy’s name, triggering blood, wails, and frantic activity; Nick, disoriented, ends up at Pennsylvania Station waiting for a train. The chapter closes with Nick attending a lavish party at Gatsby’s mansion, where he discovers Gatsby’s library contains real books, meets Gatsby himself—surprised by his rare, reassuring smile—and observes Jordan being summoned alone by Gatsby’s butler; as the party collapses into chaos—with a chorus girl weeping ink-stained tears and marriages fracturing—Nick witnesses a car crash outside Gatsby’s estate, where a dazed man emerges from a wrecked coupé, unaware of the accident, while Gatsby stands alone on the porch, waving farewell under a faint moon.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Nick Carraway</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Daisy Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Jordan Baker</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Jay Gatsby</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Myrtle Wilson</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">George Wilson</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Catherine</span>\n                    \n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">+3 more</span>\n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 2: IV</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">9,689 words &middot; 1h 4m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On a Sunday morning in West Egg, Gatsby’s mansion erupts into chaotic grandeur as guests from East Egg, New York, and beyond arrive by car—among them the Chester Beckers, Blackbucks, Dancies, and Beluga’s girls—drawn by spectacle yet ignorant of Gatsby’s true identity, while rumors swirl that he is a bootlegger, murderer, or even the devil’s kin; later, in late July, Gatsby picks up the narrator in his cream-colored car with a three-noted horn, offering a ride to Manhattan where he spins an elaborate, dubious biography of Oxford, European aristocracy, and a Montenegrin war medal, later confirmed by physical evidence, before revealing his intent to reconnect with Jordan Baker; at noon in a cellar restaurant on Forty-second Street, Gatsby introduces the narrator to Meyer Wolfshiem, a grotesque figure with human molar cuff buttons, who recounts the 1919 murder of Rosy Rosenthal and casually reveals Gatsby fixed the 1919 World Series, before Gatsby vanishes upon spotting Tom Buchanan; flashbacks reveal Daisy Fay’s 1917 encounter with Lieutenant Gatsby in Louisville, her subsequent marriage to Tom Buchanan amid a $350,000 pearl necklace and a drunken wedding-day breakdown, and her later life in Santa Barbara, France, and Chicago; in October, Jordan reveals to the narrator that Gatsby bought his mansion solely to be near Daisy’s dock, prompting the narrator to arrange a tea meeting without revealing Gatsby’s involvement; on a rain-soaked day, Gatsby, trembling and pale, reunites with Daisy at the narrator’s house, where he overwhelms her with his collection of luxurious shirts, causing her to weep; later, Gatsby shows Daisy photographs of Mr. Dan Cody and his youthful yachting self, displays newspaper clippings about her, and summons Klipspringer to play piano as they sit in shadowed intimacy, the green light on Daisy’s dock now dimmed by reality, until the narrator departs, leaving them utterly absorbed in each other.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Narrator</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Jordan Baker</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Daisy Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Meyer Wolfshiem</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Klipspringer</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Mr. Dan Cody</span>\n                    \n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">+5 more</span>\n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 3: VI</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">4,036 words &middot; 26m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with a New York reporter arriving at Gatsby’s West Egg mansion one morning, seeking a statement after hearing rumors about him, though he does not fully understand the connection. The narrative then shifts to Gatsby’s origins as James Gatz, a poor farm boy from North Dakota, who, at seventeen, rowed out to meet Dan Cody’s yacht anchored off Lake Superior, adopted the name Jay Gatsby, and spent five years working aboard the Tuolomee in roles including steward, mate, and jailor, learning to suppress his drinking due to Cody’s excesses. After Cody’s death in Boston, Ella Kaye legally deprived Gatsby of his $25,000 inheritance, leaving him with only the refined persona and education he had cultivated. Later, after weeks of separation, the narrator visits Gatsby’s mansion on a Sunday afternoon and is startled when Tom Buchanan, Mr. Sloane, and a woman in a brown riding-habit arrive on horseback; Gatsby nervously offers them drinks and invites them to supper, but they refuse and leave abruptly. That same Saturday night, Tom and Daisy attend Gatsby’s party, where Daisy is both fascinated and appalled by the raw excess of West Egg; Gatsby dances with her, and later, on the narrator’s steps, he insists she must tell Tom she never loved him to reclaim their past, clinging with religious devotion to the memory of kissing her five years ago under the moonlight, despite the narrator’s warning that the past cannot be repeated.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">James Gatz</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Dan Cody</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Ella Kaye</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">New York reporter</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Narrator</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Mr. Sloane</span>\n                    \n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">+2 more</span>\n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 4: VII</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">8,766 words &middot; 58m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On the hottest day of summer, the narrator arrives by train at the Buchanans’ house to find Gatsby waiting, having replaced his servants with Wolfsheim’s silent relatives; tension erupts when Daisy kisses Gatsby in front of Tom, who reacts with stunned disbelief, and later reveals he has investigated Gatsby’s background, dismissing his claim of attending Oxford. The group departs in two cars—Tom forces Gatsby to drive his yellow coupé while he takes Gatsby’s car—traveling first to Wilson’s garage in the Valley of Ashes under blazing afternoon sun, where George Wilson, visibly ill and emotionally withdrawn, hints he has discovered Myrtle’s secret life, while Myrtle mistakes Jordan for Tom’s wife and reacts with jealous terror. The party then proceeds to the Plaza Hotel in mid-afternoon, where a volatile argument unfolds: Tom accuses Gatsby of bootlegging through drugstores and ties to Wolfsheim, pressures Daisy to renounce her love for Tom, and she reluctantly admits she once loved him but now loves Gatsby, only to recant and say she loved both, shattering Gatsby’s idealized vision; Tom then orders Daisy and Gatsby to return to Long Island in Gatsby’s car, effectively ending the confrontation. Later, Myrtle Wilson rushes out of her garage into the dusk and is struck and killed by a speeding yellow car that fails to stop; Michaelis witnesses the aftermath, noting her fatal injuries and initially describing the car as light green. Tom and the narrator arrive at the scene to find Myrtle’s body covered on a worktable under a swinging yellow light, Wilson hysterical, and witnesses confirming the car was large and yellow; Tom denies ownership, insists his blue coupé came straight from New York, and flees the scene in tears, calling the driver a 'God damned coward.' Finally, the narrator waits outside the Buchanans’ house, where Gatsby confesses Daisy was driving the fatal car and vows to take the blame, waiting all night to protect her; Nick tiptoes to the kitchen window and sees Tom and Daisy sitting together calmly, eating cold chicken and drinking ale in quiet intimacy, confirming their reconciliation, as Nick leaves Gatsby standing alone in the moonlight, vigilantly watching a house where Daisy has already moved on.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Nick Carraway</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Jay Gatsby</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Daisy Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Jordan Baker</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">George Wilson</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Myrtle Wilson</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Michaelis</span>\n                    \n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 5: VIII</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">4,530 words &middot; 30m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the narrator, unable to sleep due to the foghorn, rushing to Gatsby’s mansion at dawn after hearing a taxi arrive, finding Gatsby dejected after waiting all night for Daisy, who briefly appeared at her window before extinguishing the light. Inside the vast, dusty house, they search for cigarettes, stumbling through unfamiliar rooms and knocking over a piano, while the narrator urges Gatsby to flee to Atlantic City or Montreal to evade suspicion over Daisy’s car—Gatsby refuses, clinging to the belief that Daisy never truly loved Tom and that their bond was sacred, even as he recounts his youthful idealization of her in Louisville and his mistaken deployment to Oxford, where he received her letter of commitment to Tom. At dawn on Long Island, the two open French windows as golden light filters in, and Gatsby reveals he never used his pool all summer; the narrator, reluctant to leave, misses two trains before departing, declaring Gatsby worth more than Tom and Daisy’s 'rotten crowd,' to which Gatsby smiles with quiet understanding. Meanwhile, in the aftermath of Myrtle Wilson’s death, George Wilson, consumed by grief and convinced of her infidelity after finding a new dog-leash, fixates on the yellow car and the eyes of Doctor T. J. Eckleburg, declaring 'God sees everything' as dawn breaks; he later travels on foot to Port Roosevelt and Gad’s Hill, buys an uneaten sandwich and coffee, and by mid-afternoon arrives in West Egg asking for Gatsby’s house. At two p.m., Gatsby, having instructed the butler to forward calls to the pool and refusing to use the damaged car, carries a pneumatic mattress alone toward the pool, where he is shot and killed by Wilson, whose body is later found in the grass nearby, completing the tragedy as the butler, chauffeur, and gardener discover Gatsby’s body floating gently in the water, motionless among leaves and ripples.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Narrator</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Daisy</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Jordan Baker</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">George Wilson</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Michaelis</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">The butler</span>\n                    \n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">+3 more</span>\n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n           