       \n  1491→                                \n  1492→                            </ul>\n  1493→                        </div>\n  1494→                    </details>\n  1495→                    \n  1496→\n  1497→                    \n  1498→                    <details style=\"margin-top: 0.5rem;\">\n  1499→                        <summary style=\"cursor: pointer; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem;\">📊 Metadata</summary>\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ce3053cf-4969-4370-b8a8-26a6f41bc92f","uuid":"eaabf09c-1a09-4b55-9c04-9243018b6c64","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/zacharymandrews/Tools/audiobook_agent/output/gatsby/report.html","content":"                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">Following Gatsby’s death, the chapter chronicles the grim aftermath as police, photographers, and reporters swarm his mansion, where sensationalized media reports label Wilson a 'madman' and Catherine falsely testifies that Myrtle was happy and never knew Gatsby. The narrator, overwhelmed by guilt, attempts to reach Daisy and Meyer Wolfsheim by phone and letter but receives no response, culminating in a chilling long-distance call where he must inform a stranger that 'Mr. Gatsby’s dead.' On the third day, Gatsby’s father, Henry C. Gatz, arrives by train from Minnesota, emotionally shattered, displaying a worn photograph of the mansion and a childhood notebook with a self-improvement schedule, while the narrator struggles to organize a funeral that draws only Gatz and a Lutheran minister—Klipspringer declines citing a picnic, and Wolfsheim refuses out of fear of implication. At five o’clock in a thick drizzle, a procession of three cars—hearse, limousine, and station wagon—arrives at the cemetery, where the mysterious man with owl-eyed glasses, unconnected to Gatsby’s circle, appears unexpectedly, wipes his glasses, and murmurs 'Amen to that' as the narrator reflects Daisy sent no message. Later, the narrator recalls his Midwestern train journeys, defining home as sensory memories of winter stations and fur coats, and decides to return West after a haunting El Greco-like vision of West Egg; he meets Jordan Baker, who reveals she is engaged, and they reconcile over their broken relationship, with the narrator admitting he is too old to deceive himself about honor. He encounters Tom Buchanan on Fifth Avenue, who admits he told Wilson Gatsby owned the car that killed Myrtle, justifying it by calling Gatsby a cruel man who 'ran over Myrtle like you'd run over a dog,' and emotionally recalls crying over dog biscuits left in his flat; the narrator shakes his hand, feeling as if speaking to a child, before Tom enters a jewelry store to buy a pearl necklace or cuff buttons. In his final visit to the abandoned mansion, the narrator erases an obscene word scrawled on the steps, avoids the taxi driver linked to the accident, and walks to the beach, reflecting on Gatsby’s futile pursuit of the green light and the enduring metaphor of humanity striving against the current, borne back into the past.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Wilson</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Catherine</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Meyer Wolfsheim</span>\n                    \n              